FILE: app/src/fly-controls.ts
```ts
import type { FreeCamera, SceneContext } from "@babylonjs/lite";
import { attachConfigurableFreeControl } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function attachFlyControls(camera: FreeCamera, canvas: HTMLCanvasElement, scene: SceneContext): () => void {
    return attachConfigurableFreeControl(camera, canvas, scene, {
        upKeys: ["Space"],
        downKeys: ["PageDown"],
        fastKeys: ["ShiftLeft", "ShiftRight"],
        fastMultiplier: 3,
    });
}
```

I used `attachConfigurableFreeControl` with explicit `upKeys` and `downKeys` to replace the defaults (which include Shift as a down key in `attachFreeControl`), so Shift is repurposed purely as a `fastKeys` speed modifier at 3×. The returned cleanup function is passed through directly from the library call, so it removes all canvas listeners and the per-frame hook.

One thing I was not sure of: whether `attachConfigurableFreeControl` is exported from the `@babylonjs/lite` package root or requires a subpath import (e.g. `@babylonjs/lite/camera/configurable-free-camera-controls`). The type declarations show it in a separate module file, but since your existing code imports `FreeCamera` and `SceneContext` from the root, I assumed the package re-exports all camera control functions from the root as well. If it's not, the import line would need to be adjusted to the subpath.
